\subsubsection*{2.2 Lazy Built-ins}

\texttt{zip}, \texttt{map}, \texttt{filter}, and \texttt{enumerate}
return lazy iterators, not lists. \texttt{zip} stops at the shortest
input; \texttt{map} stops at the shortest input when given more than one
iterable. All of them can be forced to a list with \texttt{list()}.

Subtlety: when a generator is passed to two arguments of the same lazy
consumer (e.g.\ \texttt{zip(g, g)}), each argument pulls from the same
underlying iterator, so the two output positions are interleaved.

\texttt{filter(None, xs)} keeps only truthy elements; \texttt{0},
\texttt{''}, \texttt{[]}, \texttt{None}, and \texttt{False} are all
dropped.
